A||||19||BAM-BAM||RSM's Supercharger?||||6||
Z||000000||BAM-BAM||02-06-2001||10:50 PM||||  Is RSM's Supercharger compatable with the 3.4L? I was wondering about building up a 3.4L and adding the SC, or is this a bad idea? LMK TIA. <A HREF="http://webhome.idirect.com/~rsm1/fiero_catalog.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://webhome.idirect.com/~rsm1/fiero_catalog.htm</A> <P> Peace, B2 OUT!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by BAM-BAM (edited 02-06-2001).]||||reg||6
Z||000001||Shaun41178||02-06-2001||10:58 PM||||why woulnd't it be?  the enginss are identical on the outside when installed in a fiero.  <P>In other words it will work and will make great power.||||reg||1
Z||000002||BAM-BAM||02-06-2001||11:26 PM||||  That was my thought. I, just want to be sure that they are compatable. I'm in the final decision phase of my engine swap, either a 3800sc or built 3.4L. I 'might just go with the 3.4 if I can do this type of add on later. If I can get a 3.4 up to 200hp then I should just about match the 3800sc as far as HP, no? What kind of torque could I expect? anyone? Thanks Shaun  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P> Peace, B2 OUT!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||jhogans||02-07-2001||07:58 AM||||I think you would lose your AC if you use the supercharger.  That may not be an issue but down here in Georgia, AC is a requirement  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||87ECO5||02-07-2001||09:05 AM||||I always thought the 3800sc was rated around 230-240 horsepower.  We ARE talking Series II, aren't we?<P>I read somewhere that a Design One turbocharger system (stage 1 and 2) would produce around 200 horsepower and 280 ft/lbs of torque on a 3.4 V6.  I would certainly hope a supercharger could produce more than that.<p>[This message has been edited by 87ECO5 (edited 02-09-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||elfiero||02-07-2001||09:24 AM||||I may be wrong,but I think the seriesII3800<BR>S/C HP ratings are: 91-93=230HP<BR>                    94-97=260HP<BR>                    98+  =275HP||||reg||6
Z||000006||Phil||02-07-2001||09:36 AM||||As I have said in other posts my sc3800(with Trasher racing's pulley) gives 222hp and 273 ft-lb at the wheels(5sp) auto may be less.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Shaun41178||02-07-2001||06:26 PM||||The later year 3800 SC make 240 hp.  don't know torque figures though.  Nowhere near 275 hp though<P>The first gen 3800 SC back in the early to mid 90's only made 205 peak hp.<P>A DES 1 turbo kit on a 3.4 would make more then 200 hp.  it would make in the 260 hp range with 10 psi of boost  220 hp is obtainable with a design one kit with 10 psi on a 2.8<P>------------------<BR>Shaun 85 GT with nitrous<BR>0-60 in 5.6 seconds<BR><A HREF="http://members.aol.com/Shaun41178" TARGET=_blank>Preferred Performance</A>||||reg||1
Z||000008||Shaun41178||02-07-2001||06:34 PM||||the RSm on a 3.2 or a 3.4 with around 8 psi can make about 275-300 hp.  that should be more then enough for everyday driving.  I have about 220 in mine at the flywheel and around 170 or so on the nitrous and it hauls major butt.<P>with the RSm you will lose your A/c compressor.  Who cares I say.  roll the windows down and let the 100+ mph cruising speed cool you off||||reg||1
Z||000009||Will||02-08-2001||12:21 AM||||The 3800 S/C makes 240 HP and around 280 ftlbs of torque. Check out <A HREF="http://www.gmpowertrain.com" TARGET=_blank>www.gmpowertrain.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000010||Sillhouette.com||02-08-2001||07:33 PM||||Hey an RSM supercharger question.  Great. <P>For your answer the current system is designed with the 2.8 STOCK fiero ECM.  Currently it's hooked up to a 3.2 bored out engine and a 2.9 (mine).  I'm not sure if you have to adjust the ECM tables for your 3.4.  <P>The RSM supercharger is now the VORTECH G90 kit.  Currently we have the standard (10psi) pulley but that can certainly be changed to a smaller one and give you more boost (up to 20psi).  <P>Later||||reg||1
Z||000011||87ECO5||02-09-2001||06:56 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Shaun41178:<BR><B>A DES 1 turbo kit on a 3.4 would make more then 200 hp.  it would make in the 260 hp range with 10 psi of boost  220 hp is obtainable with a design one kit with 10 psi on a 2.8</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Here's a dyno chart from Kevin Leslie.  Not sure what the PSI rating is:<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/3.4turbo.jpg"> <P>According to Kevin, this is at the wheels.<P>Of course, this whole discussion is moot since B2 is leaving us ...<p>[This message has been edited by 87ECO5 (edited 02-09-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||Shaun41178||02-09-2001||02:21 PM||||with a 15% driveline loss then the 3.4 is making about 230 hp at the flywheel.<P>That is pretty good hp and torque.  If that turbo Kevin is using is the same one that is for his 2.8 kits then more power could be generated then that with a larger turbo.  Of course we don't know what boost pressure that is at either so that does play a role as well.  If I had to guess though I would say it would be the stage 1 kit with 7 psi of boost.  Just a guess though.  Still though that is good for 0-60 times in the 5 second flat range.||||reg||1
Z||000013||lou_dias||02-09-2001||03:02 PM||||It's been my experience that the driveline loss is more than 15% on a Fiero.  A 140hp 2.8 dynos at 110hp.  That's 21.4%.  What I don't understand about driveline loss is...is it always going to be 30hp loss or is it always going to be a fixed percentage...  I'm assuming it takes 30hp just to get to the point where if an engine was trying to move the car and that engine produced 30hp, the car wouldn't move until you nudged it with your finger (I'm just being decriptive here) and at that, only with whatever added power your "finger" provided...<P>Anyway, 21.4% loss is 240 or 252 hp depending on how you did the math...  198 + 21.4 % = 240 or 198*100/(100-21.4)= 252<P>A 30hp driveline loss is 228hp...<P>Do you think I tend to over-analyze things?||||reg||1
Z||000014||HF6582||02-09-2001||03:17 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by lou_dias:<BR><B>It's been my experience that the driveline loss is more than 15% on a Fiero.  A 140hp 2.8 dynos at 110hp.  That's 21.4%.  What I don't understand about driveline loss is...is it always going to be 30hp loss or is it always going to be a fixed percentage...  I'm assuming it takes 30hp just to get to the point where if an engine was trying to move the car and that engine produced 30hp, the car wouldn't move until you nudged it with your finger (I'm just being decriptive here) and at that, only with whatever added power your "finger" provided...<P>Anyway, 21.4% loss is 240 or 252 hp depending on how you did the math...  198 + 21.4 % = 240 or 198*100/(100-21.4)= 252<P>A 30hp driveline loss is 228hp...<P>Do you think I tend to over-analyze things?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The only TRUE way to find out driveline loss, is to use an engine dyno and then a chassis dyno.<P>Loss varies car to car.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000015||ThatRickGuy||02-09-2001||03:39 PM||||Just curious on the drive train power loss...let's just say it takes 30 ftlbs to turn everyting from the fly wheel back. assuming the TC is locked. doesn't that mean that no matter what engine you use, you'll always need to use the same 29.9999 ftlbs to negate the friction/drag created by the drive train? of course the TC is going to be a major factor until it locks up, but once it's there, should it' be a certain number?||||reg||1
Z||000016||Shaun41178||02-09-2001||03:41 PM||||Driveline loss is a percentage.  I too thought it was a fixed hp but the more hp you have the more is lost through the drivetrain as it is a percentage of the flywheel hp.  if you were making 300 hp and the driveline loss was 20 percent then you would have 240 hp at the wheels||||reg||1
Z||000017||lou_dias||02-09-2001||07:24 PM||||I still don't see why it's a percentage since the coefficient of friction doesn't change as you add horsepower.  But maybe that's it.  The faster you spin something, friction fights you harder too but at the same ratio to your increase.||||reg||1
Z||000018||Will||02-09-2001||09:50 PM||||Loss isn't fixed, but it's not a fixed percentage, either. Percentage loss goes down as throughput goes up.<P>Who actually believes that a transmission can chew up 60HP without FLAT OUT MELTING?<P>There was a thread on this not too long ago:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/007992.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/007992.html</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000019||87ECO5||02-13-2001||06:29 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 87ECO5:<BR><B>Not sure what the PSI rating is:</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>"That engine was running 5-6 psig boost due to the 9.0:1 C.R." - Kevin Leslie||||reg||1
